{
  "version": 1,
  "comments": {
    "c1": {
      "anchor": "people#table-1",
      "kind": "table",
      "label": "2. Spaces and people / orgs",
      "excerpt": "Spell today AT Protocol owen@spell , a person An account (a DID, its permanent i",
      "text": "what if an org was just another space, and each member could write to anything in it, subject to permission rules we apply on the writing server?\n\nSo joining an org means you get write access to the org space.  User levels of \"viewer\", \"editor\", \"author\", \"admin\" at the org level, then again at the app level.\n\nIf spell owns the keys to write at the org level, then all writes go through spell, and it can enforce the permission model.  Yes, this means running a server, but it's pretty straightfoward, no?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-01T04:55:31.634Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "A dedicated DID that owns spaces, and can change hands without any one person's account",
      "offset": 137,
      "edited": "2026-11-01T04:58:31.559Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-01T05:05:23.594Z",
          "html": "<p>Yes, and I think this is the right shape: it removes the biggest losses (shared rows, needing an account, trusting strangers' programs).</p>\n<p>The code is straightforward. The promise isn't: Spell holds the right to write for every org, and writes stop when it's down. It's written up in \"Writing to an org's space through Spell\", with the four levels as I read them, and is now Q5 on the epic page.</p>"
        },
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-01T05:07:15.581Z",
          "html": "<p>Correction: the question on the epic page is Q6, not Q5. Q5 is the decision that each person and org has one space.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-01T05:03:17.669Z"
      }
    },
    "c2": {
      "anchor": "people#table-1",
      "kind": "table",
      "label": "2. Spaces and people / orgs",
      "excerpt": "Spell today AT Protocol owen@spell , a person An account (a DID, its permanent i",
      "text": "There is something powerful about each person having their own \"space\" and the org also having a \"space.\"  \n\nspell.spaces => myspace was a connection my partner got right away and really liked -- she remembers myspace as the first place where you could establish any identity you wanted, and liked that.  We discussed that was back when \"creating things on the web\" was fun because it wasn't so complicated, and you had the freedom to whatever you wanted (with all the downsides of ugly pages that brought).",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-01T04:58:47.400Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "An account",
      "offset": 46,
      "edited": "2026-11-01T05:00:34.352Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-01T05:05:27.835Z",
          "html": "<p>Agreed, and I've dropped my rename. Each person has a space, each org has a space, and each is one AT Protocol Space, so Spell's word and theirs now mean the same thing.</p>\n<p>The myspace idea is in \"The idea, in your words\". One thing it raises: a public page and a private draft in one space needs reads to go through Spell.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-01T05:03:19.116Z"
      }
    },
    "c3": {
      "anchor": "people#table-1",
      "kind": "table",
      "label": "2. Spaces and people / orgs",
      "excerpt": "Spell today AT Protocol owen@spell , a person An account (a DID, its permanent i",
      "text": "I guess this is literally true in terms of at protocol space, but \"a space per project\" is way too heavy and not needed.",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-01T05:01:01.772Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "A Space, named by its authority, type and key",
      "offset": 322,
      "edited": "2026-11-01T05:01:29.358Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-01T05:05:30.873Z",
          "html": "<p>Agreed: projects are records inside the space. Their design says a Space is cheap to make, so the reason is simplicity, not cost.</p>\n<p>A second Space only earns its place where the protocol must keep someone out. Otherwise it's Spell's rule, which only holds while reads go through Spell.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-01T05:03:20.452Z"
      }
    },
    "c4": {
      "anchor": "people#list-2",
      "kind": "list",
      "label": "2. Spaces and people / orgs",
      "excerpt": "The rename: Spell's \"space\" should move down a level, so owen@spell becomes your",
      "text": "right",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-01T05:01:56.165Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "A \"managed space\" asks an outside app about each person, and Spell could be that app",
      "offset": 207,
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-01T05:05:34.622Z",
          "html": "<p>Good. Spell as that app fits writing through Spell too: the server decides, whichever policy the Space uses.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-01T05:03:21.691Z"
      }
    },
    "c5": {
      "anchor": "next#list-1",
      "kind": "list",
      "label": "The new shape for the epic",
      "excerpt": "Statusphere in Spell: Bluesky's example app. Public records only, so it needs no",
      "text": "the at:.... URI business is too techy and complicated for people to use practically.  We are used to emails, and `owen@spell` reads just like `owen@spell.com` and is friendly and relatable.",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-01T05:03:06.159Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "same addressing",
      "offset": 440,
      "edited": "2026-11-01T05:04:08.404Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-01T05:07:12.545Z",
          "html": "<p>Agreed. People see <code>owen@spell</code>, and <code>at://</code> stays on the wire, with Spell keeping the permanent id behind the name. The one catch: it looks like an email address, but mail sent to it goes nowhere unless we make it work.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-01T05:06:57.154Z"
      }
    },
    "c6": {
      "anchor": "next#list-1",
      "kind": "list",
      "label": "The new shape for the epic",
      "excerpt": "Statusphere in Spell: Bluesky's example app. Public records only, so it needs no",
      "text": "Spell is the \"editing platform\", at protocol is the (or a) \"publishing platform\"?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-01T05:04:37.325Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "Seam: a Spell host stays for local and offline use",
      "offset": 367,
      "edited": "2026-11-01T05:04:53.257Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-01T05:07:14.416Z",
          "html": "<p>Mostly yes, with one addition: AT Protocol does two jobs. A Space keeps the shared data, and public records do the publishing. Spell makes, edits, runs and enforces the rules.</p>\n<p>It also gives us a real publish step, so field names only freeze when published. It's in the new section \"Editing and publishing\".</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-01T05:06:58.768Z"
      }
    },
    "c7": {
      "anchor": "people#table-1",
      "kind": "table",
      "label": "2. Spaces and people / orgs",
      "excerpt": "Spell today AT Protocol owen@spell , a person An account (a DID, its permanent i",
      "text": "even in my \"personal space,\" I might invite my partner to collaborate on a document, share a trip writup with my men's circle, etc -- so we need the \"privacy\" part of spaces with controlled access.",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-01T05:07:32.336Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "owen@spell, a person",
      "offset": 24,
      "edited": "2026-11-01T05:08:27.506Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-01T05:09:21.763Z",
          "html": "<p>Yes, and this is the one case Spaces handles badly: read access is all or nothing per Space. I'd have Spell check a list on each thing, which only holds while reads go through Spell. It's in the new section \"Sharing one thing from a space\", beside the other way.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-01T05:09:19.046Z"
      }
    }
  },
  "on": {}
}
